WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.369 --> 00:00:01.645
- People say I'm nuts.

00:00:01.645 --> 00:00:03.217
People are not waking up in the morning

00:00:03.217 --> 00:00:04.844
and saying, ooh, let me be broke.

00:00:04.844 --> 00:00:06.975
That's just a winning attitude.

00:00:06.975 --> 00:00:08.643
Why are you hanging around with that guy?

00:00:08.643 --> 00:00:10.928
Rainbows after storms.

00:00:10.928 --> 00:00:12.272
You can't fall from the floor.

00:00:12.272 --> 00:00:14.492
Confidence, well, half of success

00:00:14.492 --> 00:00:16.183
is confidence and self-esteem.

00:00:16.183 --> 00:00:17.636
The minute you think you know everything,

00:00:17.636 --> 00:00:19.203
at that moment you know nothing.

00:00:19.203 --> 00:00:21.535
If I gave the guy a
fish, he'd eat for a day.

00:00:21.535 --> 00:00:25.505
("Uptown Funk" by Mark Ronson)

00:00:25.505 --> 00:00:27.922
He's an American financial literacy

00:00:27.922 --> 00:00:29.859
entrepreneur and businessman.

00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:32.149
He's the founder, chairman, and CEO of the

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:34.656
non-profit organization HOPE.

00:00:34.656 --> 00:00:37.319
He served as vice chair on
the U.S. President's Advisory

00:00:37.319 --> 00:00:39.343
Committee for Financial Literacy.

00:00:39.343 --> 00:00:40.947
He's John Hope Bryant, and here are

00:00:40.947 --> 00:00:43.364
his Top 10 Rules for Success.

00:00:46.107 --> 00:00:49.254
People say I'm nuts,
and that's a compliment.

00:00:49.254 --> 00:00:51.977
Because they said that
Steve Jobs was nuts.

00:00:51.977 --> 00:00:53.477
They said that Ted Turner was nuts.

00:00:53.477 --> 00:00:55.585
They said that Bill Gates was nuts.

00:00:55.585 --> 00:00:56.596
And they said that my hero

00:00:56.596 --> 00:00:59.535
Dr. Martin Luther King Jr was nuts.

00:00:59.535 --> 00:01:01.421
Let me tell you what, I'll take it.

00:01:01.421 --> 00:01:02.566
People are not waking up in the morning

00:01:02.566 --> 00:01:05.316
and saying, ooh, let me be broke.

00:01:06.945 --> 00:01:11.167
Let me be poor and
ignorant, let me be stupid.

00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:15.787
Let me go into some deep,
uncomfortable waters.

00:01:15.787 --> 00:01:17.265
Because I thought I understood poverty.

00:01:17.265 --> 00:01:19.189
If you haven't noticed, I'm black.

00:01:19.189 --> 00:01:21.871
(audience laughs)

00:01:21.871 --> 00:01:24.220
I grew up in South Central
LA, in Compton, California.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.377
I'm going to tell you a
little bit about my story.

00:01:25.377 --> 00:01:28.868
But I actually thought Dr.
Franklin and I was poor.

00:01:28.868 --> 00:01:31.559
I now realize I wasn't.

00:01:31.559 --> 00:01:35.781
There's a different between
being broke and being poor.

00:01:35.781 --> 00:01:39.335
To be broke is economic, but to be poor

00:01:39.335 --> 00:01:41.668
is a disabling frame of mind

00:01:42.887 --> 00:01:45.160
and a depressed condition of your spirit

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:50.057
and you must vow never,
ever, ever to be poor again.

00:01:50.057 --> 00:01:52.307
(applause)

00:01:55.522 --> 00:01:57.138
I was talking to Dr. Franklin and others

00:01:57.138 --> 00:01:58.704
and there was an experiment
that he was telling

00:01:58.704 --> 00:02:01.127
me about about some gentlemen
who were very well-meaning,

00:02:01.127 --> 00:02:02.651
they were going out, hanging
out with homeless people

00:02:02.651 --> 00:02:05.026
to try and relate to their experience.

00:02:05.026 --> 00:02:06.921
They dressed like them, they
act like, they walk like them,

00:02:06.921 --> 00:02:08.731
they talk like them, the live like them,

00:02:08.731 --> 00:02:10.810
but the legitimate people who are homeless

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:12.727
knew that these were instigators.

00:02:12.727 --> 00:02:14.436
They knew that they were not homeless.

00:02:14.436 --> 00:02:17.769
Why? The twinkle was still in their eye.

00:02:20.552 --> 00:02:23.302
The hope was still in their eyes.

00:02:24.981 --> 00:02:26.820
I can give a homeless
guy a million dollars,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.205
he'll be broke in six months.

00:02:29.205 --> 00:02:30.438
With the best of intentions, because

00:02:30.438 --> 00:02:33.496
nothing changed here, and here.

00:02:33.496 --> 00:02:37.740
The word capital comes from
the Latin root word capitas.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.840
It means knowledge in the head.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.372
It doesn't even mean money.

00:02:41.372 --> 00:02:43.951
I told you, the issue is confidence.

00:02:43.951 --> 00:02:46.900
But if I can make you immune to failure,

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.325
not ashamed to trip and fall.

00:02:49.325 --> 00:02:52.130
If you can have an
opportunity to have somebody

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:55.329
invest in you at an early
age, believe in yourself,

00:02:55.329 --> 00:02:58.572
have some experience with free enterprise,

00:02:58.572 --> 00:03:01.301
with starting something on your
own, believing in yourself,

00:03:01.301 --> 00:03:04.460
pursuing it, no matter what the outcome.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.993
An entrepreneurial mindset works

00:03:06.993 --> 00:03:09.166
whether you're running a household

00:03:09.166 --> 00:03:12.086
or whether you're running
a community center,

00:03:12.086 --> 00:03:16.253
or city government, or your
own life, or your own business.

00:03:17.115 --> 00:03:20.032
It is the can do empowerment-based,

00:03:21.837 --> 00:03:25.470
what I'm for not what I'm against mindset

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:29.155
that I will do anything from anywhere

00:03:29.155 --> 00:03:31.445
for any purpose at any time

00:03:31.445 --> 00:03:34.489
that is unique to an
entrepreneur, that is always

00:03:34.489 --> 00:03:37.549
reimagining everything
and creating solutions.

00:03:37.549 --> 00:03:40.764
That's just a winning attitude about life.

00:03:40.764 --> 00:03:42.841
So here's a rule for you,
talk without being offensive,

00:03:42.841 --> 00:03:45.162
listen without being defensive, but always

00:03:45.162 --> 00:03:48.424
leave even your adversary
with their dignity.

00:03:48.424 --> 00:03:49.661
Because if you don't, they'll spend

00:03:49.661 --> 00:03:51.735
the rest of their life
trying to make you miserable.

00:03:51.735 --> 00:03:53.004
So help people win.

00:03:53.004 --> 00:03:55.257
When George Bush was in
office, my friends, oh,

00:03:55.257 --> 00:03:56.752
why are you hanging around with that guy?

00:03:56.752 --> 00:03:58.280
So what do I say? He's the president.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.666
(audience laughs)

00:04:01.666 --> 00:04:03.954
I talked to him for a while and I got him

00:04:03.954 --> 00:04:06.407
to sign his executive order
around financial literacy,

00:04:06.407 --> 00:04:08.871
and I'm proud that he did that.

00:04:08.871 --> 00:04:12.386
And by the way, I've honored
to serve now three presidents

00:04:12.386 --> 00:04:13.795
from both political parties, Republicans

00:04:13.795 --> 00:04:17.250
and Democrats, I serve Obama today.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:18.539
But I think we need to talk to everybody,

00:04:18.539 --> 00:04:19.939
and you never know who your
friends are going to be,

00:04:19.939 --> 00:04:23.167
you should make friends everywhere.

00:04:23.167 --> 00:04:27.177
People who you least
expect to provide the sort

00:04:27.177 --> 00:04:29.605
of leadership you need may
be the ones that actually

00:04:29.605 --> 00:04:32.833
end up doing it, so
make friends everywhere.

00:04:32.833 --> 00:04:34.513
You cannot grow without
legitimate suffering.

00:04:34.513 --> 00:04:37.230
You cannot grow without
legitimate suffering.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:38.486
Rainbows follow storms.

00:04:38.486 --> 00:04:41.819
My last book, the favorite
chapter was Loss Creates Leaders.

00:04:41.819 --> 00:04:43.265
Nobody woke up in the
morning and said I want

00:04:43.265 --> 00:04:45.475
to start a cancer foundation.

00:04:45.475 --> 00:04:48.457
People who start cancer
foundations are people

00:04:48.457 --> 00:04:51.361
whose lives have been
impacted somehow by cancer.

00:04:51.361 --> 00:04:53.194
Rainbows after storms.

00:04:54.495 --> 00:04:56.530
I was homeless for six months in my life.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:59.199
- And what did that teach you?

00:04:59.199 --> 00:05:01.438
- You can't fall from the floor.

00:05:01.438 --> 00:05:03.697
That I had to redefine
failure as going from,

00:05:03.697 --> 00:05:06.258
redefine success, actually,
from going from failure

00:05:06.258 --> 00:05:08.823
to failure without loss of enthusiasm.

00:05:08.823 --> 00:05:11.286
That I had to learn to do for myself.

00:05:11.286 --> 00:05:14.750
That PhDs were good,
but PH dos were better.

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:19.538
And it made me get real
with success and failure

00:05:19.538 --> 00:05:21.913
in my life and made me resilient.

00:05:21.913 --> 00:05:23.834
For you to sit here and ask me questions

00:05:23.834 --> 00:05:25.939
or approach me this morning
takes personal confidence.

00:05:25.939 --> 00:05:27.227
For you to look me in the eye right

00:05:27.227 --> 00:05:29.340
now takes personal confidence.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:32.776
Well, half of success is
confidence and self-esteem.

00:05:32.776 --> 00:05:34.913
But if you're poor,
you've been beaten down,

00:05:34.913 --> 00:05:36.523
you've been depressed, you lack hope.

00:05:36.523 --> 00:05:38.405
So it's very interesting,
the very thing you need

00:05:38.405 --> 00:05:41.144
as the first option to be successful

00:05:41.144 --> 00:05:42.982
you actually don't have
by virtue of the fact

00:05:42.982 --> 00:05:45.484
that you've been in this poor environment.

00:05:45.484 --> 00:05:49.826
Today my life makes sense,
but the minute you think

00:05:49.826 --> 00:05:52.353
you know everything, at that
moment you know nothing.

00:05:52.353 --> 00:05:56.539
I was in India and I was
teaching a course on leadership

00:05:56.539 --> 00:06:00.943
in India, and I had gone to
this university and I spoke

00:06:00.943 --> 00:06:05.071
there, and after I left,
I got back to the hotel

00:06:05.071 --> 00:06:07.268
and I realized I didn't have my wallet.

00:06:07.268 --> 00:06:09.327
I called the hotel and
I said would you please

00:06:09.327 --> 00:06:10.938
bring the car back, they have my wallet.

00:06:10.938 --> 00:06:12.927
They said, "I'm sorry,
sir, we don't have a car."

00:06:12.927 --> 00:06:15.192
"That was a taxi we used."

00:06:15.192 --> 00:06:16.690
They tried calling the taxi company

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:17.765
and nobody answered the phone.

00:06:17.765 --> 00:06:20.398
They tried again, nobody
answered the phone.

00:06:20.398 --> 00:06:22.753
I became a little cynical.

00:06:22.753 --> 00:06:24.527
I went from hopeful, to
skeptical, to cynical,

00:06:24.527 --> 00:06:25.869
and I started thinking
the worst of people.

00:06:25.869 --> 00:06:29.300
And of course I said,
well, my wallet is gone.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:31.745
By midnight they called and
said we're still trying,

00:06:31.745 --> 00:06:33.151
he's not answering the
phone, the taxi cab driver.

00:06:33.151 --> 00:06:34.906
But at two in the morning
they called and said

00:06:34.906 --> 00:06:38.904
he answered his phone, his phone was off,

00:06:38.904 --> 00:06:41.714
he's coming back to your
hotel with your wallet.

00:06:41.714 --> 00:06:44.814
So I get dressed and I run
downstairs and a man meets

00:06:44.814 --> 00:06:48.380
me and the bellman
translates for me in Indian,

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:51.689
in the dialect, and I get my wallet

00:06:51.689 --> 00:06:54.511
and everything is in
my wallet, everything.

00:06:54.511 --> 00:06:58.161
My passport, my money, my
credit cards, it's all there.

00:06:58.161 --> 00:07:01.558
I reach into my wallet and there's $70 US.

00:07:01.558 --> 00:07:03.182
I ask the man, through a translator,

00:07:03.182 --> 00:07:04.080
what do you make a month?

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.206
He says about 2500 rupees, which,

00:07:06.206 --> 00:07:08.789
at that time, was about $70 US.

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:14.056
I reach out and I say let
me give this $70 to you,

00:07:14.056 --> 00:07:17.574
this is my way of saying thank you.

00:07:17.574 --> 00:07:19.646
And the man says no.

00:07:19.646 --> 00:07:21.525
I said, no, I don't think you understand.

00:07:21.525 --> 00:07:23.611
I have one month's salary for you,

00:07:23.611 --> 00:07:25.479
I want to say thank you, here's $70.

00:07:25.479 --> 00:07:27.992
The man says to the translator, "No."

00:07:27.992 --> 00:07:32.180
I said there must be a
translation problem here.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:34.175
I'm talking about paying you,
I'm talking about giving you

00:07:34.175 --> 00:07:36.277
a month's worth of your salary.

00:07:36.277 --> 00:07:38.564
One more time, and this
time he became a little

00:07:38.564 --> 00:07:41.647
visibly angry, he said I told you no.

00:07:43.631 --> 00:07:48.144
I didn't bring you your wallet
because I wanted a reward.

00:07:48.144 --> 00:07:52.311
I brought you your wallet
because it's your wallet.

00:07:54.506 --> 00:07:58.185
Now this may not sound
like an incredible story,

00:07:58.185 --> 00:07:59.933
but think what would've
happened if I'd lost

00:07:59.933 --> 00:08:03.337
my wallet in a taxi in New York City.

00:08:03.337 --> 00:08:05.510
Or a big city anywhere in the world.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:07.763
Think about the chances of
me getting my wallet back.

00:08:07.763 --> 00:08:09.504
Think about it and be
honest with yourself, now.

00:08:09.504 --> 00:08:12.754
This is a slum, they call it, in India.

00:08:14.636 --> 00:08:16.830
So I said to the man what can
I do to be thankful to you?

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:18.640
What can I do to say thank you?

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.247
He said well the next
time you're in India,

00:08:21.247 --> 00:08:24.914
come to my house and
have tea, be my friend.

00:08:27.582 --> 00:08:30.749
Whoo. Sometimes when you give you get.

00:08:33.051 --> 00:08:36.718
Sometimes whatever goes
around comes around.

00:08:39.357 --> 00:08:41.770
And so ubuntu, as they
say in South Africa.

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:44.853
Ubuntu, I am me because you are you.

00:08:45.871 --> 00:08:47.701
I didn't give that man a thing,

00:08:47.701 --> 00:08:49.906
he gave me a sense of purpose.

00:08:49.906 --> 00:08:51.445
He let me understand that there's hope

00:08:51.445 --> 00:08:53.808
in people and that you can believe

00:08:53.808 --> 00:08:55.819
in the humanity and the decency of people.

00:08:55.819 --> 00:08:56.996
And sometimes people just do things

00:08:56.996 --> 00:08:59.689
because it's the right thing to do.

00:08:59.689 --> 00:09:01.637
What if we all just made a commitment

00:09:01.637 --> 00:09:03.905
to be someone's friend,
to show up in their house

00:09:03.905 --> 00:09:05.575
and have tea, or better, to invite them

00:09:05.575 --> 00:09:08.193
into our house and make that connection.

00:09:08.193 --> 00:09:11.276
You gain more by giving, you get more

00:09:12.147 --> 00:09:15.564
by building, you learn more by listening.

00:09:17.076 --> 00:09:19.491
We have a chance and an
opportunity to change the world

00:09:19.491 --> 00:09:21.962
because nobody changes in good times.

00:09:21.962 --> 00:09:25.433
People only change in bad times.

00:09:25.433 --> 00:09:27.082
No one changes when they're comfortable,

00:09:27.082 --> 00:09:30.332
they chance when they're uncomfortable.

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:35.221
We have a chance now to change the world.

00:09:35.221 --> 00:09:37.623
So here's my question as I wrap up,

00:09:37.623 --> 00:09:41.262
and I'm now going to come right to you.

00:09:41.262 --> 00:09:45.917
I'm going to ask you what kind
of bird do you want to be.

00:09:45.917 --> 00:09:48.642
But I want you to answer in your own

00:09:48.642 --> 00:09:51.187
dialect, in your own word,
but I'm going to start

00:09:51.187 --> 00:09:55.580
with something easy and you're
going to repeat after me.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:57.497
I can change the world.

00:09:58.732 --> 00:10:02.028
- [Audience] I can change the world

00:10:02.028 --> 00:10:05.489
- I will be the change in the world.

00:10:05.489 --> 00:10:08.241
- [Audience] I will be
the change in the world.

00:10:08.241 --> 00:10:10.740
- You guys sound really weak.

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:12.448
(laughter)

00:10:12.448 --> 00:10:14.542
Talk like you got passion in your life.

00:10:14.542 --> 00:10:15.375
(scream)

00:10:15.375 --> 00:10:16.324
Oh, my god.

00:10:16.324 --> 00:10:18.574
(applause)

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:24.909
Remember now, you're honorary black

00:10:24.909 --> 00:10:28.279
people, you got to have some soul.

00:10:28.279 --> 00:10:30.774
I'm going to ask you one more time.

00:10:30.774 --> 00:10:31.941
I am somebody.

00:10:32.918 --> 00:10:35.029
- [Audience] I am somebody.

00:10:35.029 --> 00:10:36.831
- I can change the world.

00:10:36.831 --> 00:10:38.717
- [Audience] I can change the world.

00:10:38.717 --> 00:10:40.100
- I am

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:41.380
- [Audience] I am

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:42.246
- I

00:10:42.246 --> 00:10:44.117
- [Audience] I

00:10:44.117 --> 00:10:46.832
- Question, what kind of
bird do you want to be?

00:10:46.832 --> 00:10:48.371
- [Audience] An eagle.

00:10:48.371 --> 00:10:49.853
- No, no, say it with passion.

00:10:49.853 --> 00:10:52.005
What kind of bird do you want to be?

00:10:52.005 --> 00:10:53.510
- [Audience] An eagle.

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:55.290
- Give them a round of applause.

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:57.540
(applause)

00:11:01.693 --> 00:11:04.863
So if I gave the guy a fish,
he'd eat for a day, okay?

00:11:04.863 --> 00:11:06.778
But I taught him how to fish.

00:11:06.778 --> 00:11:08.997
Not only is he eating for a day,

00:11:08.997 --> 00:11:10.584
not only is his family eating,

00:11:10.584 --> 00:11:12.768
his employees are
eating, he's actually now

00:11:12.768 --> 00:11:14.599
teaching other people how to eat.

00:11:14.599 --> 00:11:16.568
And that ripple effect.

00:11:16.568 --> 00:11:19.886
Financial literacy is
the only philanthropic

00:11:19.886 --> 00:11:22.836
or charitable or non-profit activity

00:11:22.836 --> 00:11:25.216
that has leverage, that uses leverage.

00:11:25.216 --> 00:11:28.885
Once I teach you, you're never untaught.

00:11:28.885 --> 00:11:30.521
Once your rubber band is expanded,

00:11:30.521 --> 00:11:32.765
it doesn't go back to the
same size, it's expanded.

00:11:32.765 --> 00:11:36.420
So your understanding,
your appreciation for life

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:40.587
is different, and that is
the special sauce, to me,

00:11:41.969 --> 00:11:46.609
that is what makes this
transformational in its potential.

00:11:46.609 --> 00:11:49.026
I was a dancer on Soul Train.

00:11:50.302 --> 00:11:52.552
(laughter)

00:11:54.966 --> 00:11:56.873
And American Bandstand.

00:11:56.873 --> 00:11:57.896
- Wow.

00:11:57.896 --> 00:12:01.646
- They taped across the
hall from each other.

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:06.020
I've never done this publicly.

00:12:07.001 --> 00:12:08.484
Is there any music?

00:12:08.484 --> 00:12:10.915
- [Interviewer] I'm going
to get out of the way.

00:12:10.915 --> 00:12:11.748
(disco music)

00:12:11.748 --> 00:12:12.581
- Yes, please.

00:12:12.581 --> 00:12:13.786
I hope none of my
shareholders are watching.

00:12:13.786 --> 00:12:15.953
My investment may go down.

00:12:17.213 --> 00:12:18.960
No, no, no, no.

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:21.255
I need something with some beat to it.

00:12:21.255 --> 00:12:22.994
That is far too...

00:12:22.994 --> 00:12:25.244
(laughter)

00:12:26.085 --> 00:12:27.430
That actually might have been like the

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:30.751
theme music for Soul Train, I think.

00:12:30.751 --> 00:12:31.970
You know, I don't have to do this.

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:35.303
You can just cut the music all together.

00:12:36.183 --> 00:12:40.519
("Uptown Funk" by Mark Ronson)

00:12:40.519 --> 00:12:41.881
(clapping)

00:12:41.881 --> 00:12:44.136
I don't need all that.

00:12:44.136 --> 00:12:48.303
I've got many problems,
self-esteem is not one of them.

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:04.880
(cheering)

00:13:16.037 --> 00:13:17.775
I'm sure I'll be paying
the price for that later.

00:13:17.775 --> 00:13:21.151
(audience laughs)

00:13:21.151 --> 00:13:24.279
Luckily, that show kept me in shape

00:13:24.279 --> 00:13:27.946
and I stay in shape,
so I have minimal pain.

00:13:30.554 --> 00:13:34.721
I'm sure that was pretty
unique amongst your speakers.

00:13:37.151 --> 00:13:39.377
That pretty much falls in place

00:13:39.377 --> 00:13:42.907
with my life philosophy,
which is take life seriously

00:13:42.907 --> 00:13:46.074
but don't take yourself too seriously.

00:13:49.741 --> 00:13:51.324
Have a light touch.

00:13:52.612 --> 00:13:54.193
One of the diseases that we have

00:13:54.193 --> 00:13:56.732
in this world is seriousness.

00:13:56.732 --> 00:13:59.880
People take themselves very serious.

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:01.409
People who take themselves
very serious enforce

00:14:01.409 --> 00:14:05.576
other people to also suffer
from this disease of insecurity,

00:14:08.288 --> 00:14:10.023
which is then connected to fear,

00:14:10.023 --> 00:14:12.340
which is then tied to how
you treat other people.

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:13.561
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:14:13.561 --> 00:14:16.309
I made this video because
Bishop asked me to.

00:14:16.309 --> 00:14:17.624
If there's a famous entrepreneur that you

00:14:17.624 --> 00:14:19.945
want me to profile next, leave it

00:14:19.945 --> 00:14:22.337
in the comments below, and
I'll see what I can do.

00:14:22.337 --> 00:14:24.684
I'd also love to know
which of John Hope Bryant's

00:14:24.684 --> 00:14:28.213
Top 10 Rules meant the most to
you, had the biggest impact.

00:14:28.213 --> 00:14:30.556
Leave it in the comments and
I'll join the discussion.

00:14:30.556 --> 00:14:31.854
Thank you so much for watching,

00:14:31.854 --> 00:14:35.521
continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

